1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:04,400
SelectFlow: a portal to compose workflows across public bodies.

2
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:09,000
The home page reaches every function in one click.

3
00:00:09,350 --> 00:00:13,200
The button at the top right opens the “Language model” page.

4
00:00:13,450 --> 00:00:17,300
Choose a provider: here Claude, available in claude.ai.

5
00:00:17,550 --> 00:00:21,400
Then the model: Haiku, Sonnet or Opus 5.5.

6
00:00:21,650 --> 00:00:25,900
“Test” checks the call; “Use this model” enables it for the whole portal.

7
00:00:26,350 --> 00:00:30,000
To use an API key, choose “Anthropic API”.

8
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:34,000
Paste the key: it is kept in this tab only.

9
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:38,600
A page published on claude.ai cannot reach other providers: download it.

10
00:00:38,850 --> 00:00:40,950
Then “Use this model”.

11
00:00:41,350 --> 00:00:45,400
Even without a model, 95 example requests are ready to run.

12
00:00:45,650 --> 00:00:49,600
Filter by country, number of tasks or language.

13
00:00:49,850 --> 00:00:54,900
“Run” derives the workflow and composes the toolkits at once.

14
00:00:55,350 --> 00:00:59,200
Or write your own request in everyday language.

15
00:00:59,450 --> 00:01:02,500
Then submit it.

16
00:01:02,850 --> 00:01:06,600
Interpretation proposes the target documents, with or without a model.

17
00:01:06,850 --> 00:01:10,900
Correct them if needed, then confirm: nothing is derived before that.

18
00:01:11,350 --> 00:01:15,900
The verified workflow appears as an activity diagram, one lane per body.

19
00:01:16,150 --> 00:01:19,400
Here it matches the registry’s workflow exactly.

20
00:01:19,750 --> 00:01:23,000
Candidates, critics and forward chaining remain available.

21
00:01:23,250 --> 00:01:26,900
Derive with the chosen model, or without one, then confirm.

22
00:01:27,350 --> 00:01:32,400
Each task gets a model host and a toolkit: the tools offered to the agent.

23
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:36,900
The percentages predict which tool the agent will pick from each toolkit.

24
00:01:37,150 --> 00:01:40,600
“Compose again” recomputes with the current parameters.

25
00:01:40,850 --> 00:01:44,400
“Ask the live agent” offers the toolkit to the model and records the tool it calls.

26
00:01:44,650 --> 00:01:46,950
Several plans are compared.

27
00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:51,600
The final plan shows the host and the toolkit of every task.

28
00:01:51,850 --> 00:01:55,200
Constraints C1 to C6 are validated.

29
00:01:55,450 --> 00:01:58,950
Save the plan, or export it as JSON.

30
00:01:59,350 --> 00:02:03,000
Bodies and servers: 32 public bodies and their MCP servers.

31
00:02:03,250 --> 00:02:06,400
Each profile shows its tools, procedures and agreements.

32
00:02:06,750 --> 00:02:10,900
The servers table shows endpoint, status and number of tools.

33
00:02:11,150 --> 00:02:13,950
“Add server” adds one.

34
00:02:14,350 --> 00:02:18,200
The catalog: 308 tools, with search and filters.

35
00:02:18,450 --> 00:02:21,600
Body, capability, certification, latency, dependability.

36
00:02:21,950 --> 00:02:26,000
A tool’s profile shows its card, its behaviour and its schemas.

37
00:02:26,250 --> 00:02:28,950
Edit it, delete it, or import tools.

38
00:02:29,350 --> 00:02:33,600
The MCP connectors of your claude.ai account are listed here.

39
00:02:33,850 --> 00:02:38,950
Describe their tools, test a call and import them into the catalog.

40
00:02:39,350 --> 00:02:42,800
The matrix shows the flows allowed between bodies.

41
00:02:43,050 --> 00:02:45,600
One click gives the reason.

42
00:02:45,950 --> 00:02:51,950
The history groups each request with its operations, ready to export.
